, since B1-B2-B3 is more probable than keeping B1-B3 which wastes 1 extra tile.\nJ2 is safe, it doesn't do anything for me right now. No one will want to take that one.\n\n\n\n", "output": "Evaluation: The current hand contains a meld [T1, T2, T3], two completed chows in the Character suit ([W1, W2, W3] and [W4, W5, W6]), a pair of T9, and an isolated J2, plus an incomplete shape [B1, B3]. \nThe most promising scoring pattern is **Mixed Triple Chow** (8 points), which requires forming [B1, B2, B3] to match the [T1, T2, T3] and [W1, W2, W3] chows. Although B2 is scarce (with 3 visible on the board and 1 listed as unshown), retaining B1 and B3 keeps this winning condition alive.\nAlternatively, the hand could pivot to a **Pure Straight** in the Character suit (16 points) if the tiles W7, W8, and W9 are acquired.\nThe tile J2 is an isolated honor (Green Dragon) that does not fit into either strategy and prevents potential points from \"All Chows\" and \"No Honors\". Discarding J2 is the safest and most efficient move to progress the hand.\n\nReason: J2 is an isolated honor tile that contributes no points and hinders the formation of \"All Chows\" and \"No Honors\" patterns. Discarding it maintains the 1-shanten status for the high-scoring Mixed Triple Chow (waiting on B2) while also allowing a pivot to a Pure Straight if Character tiles (W7, W8, W9) are drawn.\n\nAnswer: Play J2"}
